    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d139">

                <pb facs="0279" n="265" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg265"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EAC/8–944: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-08-09T13:00:00+02:00">August 9, 1944—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 9—8:23 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>6370. For the Acting Secretary. In regard to second paragraph of your 6254 of
                    August 8 I want you to know that the Russians on the European Advisory
                    Commission are not aware that we intend to inform the Chinese in regard to the
                    surrender terms. The conversations within the EAC bind us to absolute secrecy
                    until notice and consent are agreed to by the three parties represented.</p>
                <p>In relation to this same problem I want to warn you against the Chinese codes
                    which I believe are unsafe and probably known to the Germans as well as to our
                    two Allies.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
